Task: Verify account-deletion invariants still hold (Phase 2, Tier 1)
Track: fy_tax_pack_20260729

SUMMARY
Migration 058 redefines user_storage_objects() and
property_storage_objects() to include loan_statements.storage_path and
depreciation_reports.storage_path. docs/account-deletion.md updated.

WHY THIS TASK EXISTS
Storage has no DB cascade. docs/account-deletion.md states the
maintenance rule: every column holding a storage path must be enumerated
in BOTH functions or deletion leaks the files. This phase added two
file-bearing tables, so skipping this would mean loan statements and QS
reports survive an account deletion - a privacy failure, not untidiness.

KEY INVARIANT PRESERVED
Both branches resolve ownership through properties.user_id, never by the
uploader path prefix. A co-owner guest can upload a loan statement into
the owner's property: the object then sits under the GUEST's id prefix
while the row belongs to the OWNER. A prefix scan would either delete the
owner's surviving files or miss them.

depreciation_reports.storage_path is intentionally non-unique - one QS
report covers several financial years - so that branch uses SELECT
DISTINCT.

VERIFICATION PERFORMED (live, local Supabase)
npm run db:reset -> 58 migrations replayed cleanly.

Targeted check, with a loan statement deliberately stored under the
GUEST's id prefix and one QS report cited by three financial years:
  user_storage_objects(owner)          -> returns both new files
  property_storage_objects(property)   -> returns both new files
  QS report entries (3 rows, one path) -> 1 entry (DISTINCT works)
  loan file in OWNER's set             -> 1
  loan file in GUEST's set             -> 0
The last two are the invariant: attribution follows data ownership, not
the path prefix.

npm run verify:deletion -> ALL CHECKS PASSED (14 assertions), covering
requirement 2 (guest deletes account: own files removed, uploads into the
owner's property preserved, no ghost membership row) and requirement 1b
(owner deletes property: all its files removed, including the
guest-uploaded one).

npm run build     -> Compiled successfully.
CI=true npm test  -> 41 tests passed.

NOTE
verify:deletion does not yet stage loan_statements or depreciation_reports
files of its own - it exercises the pre-existing buckets. The targeted
check above covers the new tables directly. Extending the harness to stage
them would make the regression permanent and is worth doing when those
upload paths are built in Phase 3.
